s batching the `eq_ub` transfers instead of doing separate H2D copies for each window.\n\nSince degenerate windows may be scattered throughout the full window list with non-degenerate ones interspersed, the `eq_ub_per_trace` slices aren't contiguous in memory. I can either gather just the degenerate windows' `eq_ub` data into one buffer for a single upload, or upload the entire `eq_ub_per_trace` array and let each block index into it using its window offsets.\n\nOption (b) is cleaner—I'll just upload the full `eq_ub_per_trace` array once per round since it's already the same size as `unstacked_cols`, which is already on device. The current `d_eq_ub` buffer is sized for `max_window_len`, so I'd need to expand it for the batched approach, but uploading the complete array avoids the complexity of CPU-side gathering and lets each block use its natural window boundaries. Looking at how `ht_diff_idxs` is constructed and how `sorted_cols` orders traces by height in descending order, I can see that non-degenerate windows with larger heights come first in the array, followed by degenerate windows with smaller heights at the end. This spatial locality could simplify the batching strategy. Wait, that depends on the iteration order over commits and traces. Let me look more carefully...\n\nIn `new()`, the loop iterates through commits, and within each commit through traces, building up `ht_diff_idxs` with one entry per trace. The layout is sorted by height within each commit (largest first), but heights can interleave across commits.\n\nFor the eq_ub batching, I should gather the degenerate windows' eq_ub values into a contiguous buffer on the host side, then upload that along with metadata. I can classify windows as degenerate or non-degenerate, batch the degenerate ones into a single kernel launch, and keep the existing approach for non-degenerate windows.\n\nFor the metadata struct, I need offsets into the global `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` arrays, plus an offset into 