acked reduction sumcheck MLE round kernel with the appropriate parameters, then transferring the accumulated results back to host memory and reducing them to field elements before adding to the totals. Both kernels use `stacked_height` as the `q_height` parameter, and since it's consistent across all windows in a round, I only need to compute it once. For error handling, I can reuse the existing `SumcheckMleRoundDegenerate` error variant since the batched kernel serves the same purpose.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the `d_eq_ub` buffer allocation strategy. Currently it's allocated with capacity for the largest single window, but in the batched approach it needs to hold all degenerate eq_ub values at once, which could be much larger—potentially the sum of all degenerate window lengths. The buffer is still bounded by the total number of columns across all commits, and the existing resize logic should handle cases where the total degenerate eq_ub exceeds the current capacity. Still writing buffer allocation...\n\nThe resize happens inside the window loop, allocating space for all degenerate eq_ub values in one shot per round. Now I'm looking at the device memory footprint for the metadata structures — metadata, rotation values, and equation bounds each take around 160KB per round for roughly 10K degenerate windows.\n\nThese buffers get allocated and freed each round via stream-ordered operations, which is efficient enough. I could cache them across rounds to avoid repeated allocation overhead, but the ~500KB cost is negligible compared to the kernel launch savings we're getting. For the prototype, keeping it simple makes sense.\n\nLooking back at the task requirements, `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` are already contiguous on device, so I just need to pass them through to the batched kernel.  The suggestion is to pre-upload concatenated `eq_ub` values in a single transfer and use a metadata struct containing the window offset, length, and the field extension values. This is cleaner than managing separate device arrays, though I need to verify the alignment works out properly. `FpExt` appears to be a straightforward struct of field elements without special alignment requirements based on how it's used across the FFI boundary.\n\nDefining the metadata struct... Adjusting the struct to 48 bytes for better GPU memory alignment—padding the three u32 fields to 16 bytes, then placing the two FpExt fields at 16-byte boundaries for efficient memory coalescing, even though all threads in a block will broadcast-read the same metadata entry anyway.\n\nI should also verify that the maximum number of degenerate windows (around 10,348 at APC 300) won't exceed the 65535 grid dimension limit, and double-check the FpExt alignment assumptions before finalizing the implementation plan.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7250,"cache_read_input_tokens":53082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7250},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f75a9bb0-08dc-4cc2-8653-93cb45f6f821","uuid":"4e6c797a-cf2e-4ed7-a78a-38c8ffcb2d70"}
