{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UBKAq7nXaM9TKpZaaoXJgD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review Guideline\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReview an implementation plan against the current codebase and existing measurements.\n6\t\n7\tThe job is not to paraphrase the plan. The job is to find:\n8\t\n9\t- correctness holes\n10\t- missing invariants\n11\t- interface mismatches with the current code\n12\t- places where estimated wins are not supported by measurements\n13\t- places where the plan is overcomplicated relative to expected proof-time benefit\n14\t- possible simplifications\n15\t\n16\t## Core standard\n17\t\n18\tA good review answers four questions:\n19\t\n20\t1. Is the proposal correct against the code that exists today?\n21\t2. Is it specific enough to implement a prototype without inventing missing design later?\n22\t3. Is it likely to improve the measured bottleneck it claims to target?\n23\t4. Is the proposed complexity justified by expected wall-clock benefit?\n24\t\n25\t## Review method\n26\t\n27\t### 1. Read the plan for concrete claims\n28\t\n29\tExtract:\n30\t\n31\t- the claimed bottleneck\n32\t- the proposed mechanism\n33\t- what is said to be AIR-level vs trace-level\n34\t- what is said to be reused, cached, batched, or hoisted\n35\t- expected savings and what units they use\n36\t\n37\tDo not accept vague claims like “should help” or “future cleanup will handle this.”\n38\t\n39\t### 2. Check the current code path end to end\n40\t\n41\tFind the actual call chain in the current implementation:\n42\t\n43\t- caller\n44\t- helper\n45\t- FFI boundary\n46\t- CUDA/kernel side if relevant\n47\t- postprocess / D2H / synchronization points\n48\t\n49\tVerify that the plan matches the real architecture, not an assumed one.\n50\t\n51\t### 3. Check measurements before trusting priorities\n52\t\n53\tUse existing profiling or instrumentation first.\n54\t\n55\tFor each claimed win, ask:\n56\t\n57\t- is this work actually inside the measured bottleneck?\n58\t- is the cited span wall-clock or only a subspan?\n59\t- is the estimate per segment, per round, or per proof?\n60\t\n61\tReject priority arguments that contradict the data.\n62\t\n63\t### 4. Separate design blockers from cleanup\n64\t\n65\tA design blocker is something that makes the proposal:\n66\t\n67\t- incorrect\n68\t- unimplementable as written\n69\t- likely to fail at runtime\n70\t- likely to miss the intended performance target\n71\t\n72\tCleanup issues are:\n73\t\n74\t- wording imprecision\n75\t- stale checklist items\n76\t- missing explicit `Default` impl mention\n77\t- test/checklist polish\n78\t\n79\tBe strict about the difference.\n80\t\n81\t## What to verify every time\n82\t\n83\t### Correctness / architecture\n84\t\n85\t- Does the proposal preserve current invariants?\n86\t- Does it account for all inputs needed to reproduce current behavior?\n87\t- Does it match current buffer layout, indexing, launch geometry, and reduction shape?\n88\t- Does it accidentally rely on information only available on the CUDA side or only on the Rust side?\n89\t- Does it handle fallback paths, not just the new fast path?\n90\t\n91\t### Memory / buffers\n92\t\n93\t- Is capacity confused with logical length?\n94\t- Are reused buffers read back to host with the correct live length?\n95\t- Does the plan assume async frees happen immediately?\n96\t- Is the memory budget a real bound or just a heuristic?\n97\t- If sub-batching is proposed, is the scope of reuse actually large enough to matter?\n98\t\n99\t### Performance realism\n100\t\n101\t- Is the plan attacking the span that is actually large in Nsight/NVTX?\n102\t- Are claimed savings plausible relative to the cited measured span?\n103\t- Is the proposal removing work, or only moving it around?\n104\t- Is the proposal saving GPU time, CPU time, runtime API overhead, or sync time? Be explicit.\n105\t\n106\t### Implementation readiness\n107\t\n108\t- Are required new structs/helpers/FFI changes fully specified?\n109\t- Are raw pointers, lifetimes, or ownership changes spelled out?\n110\t- Does the checklist include all touched paths?\n111\t- Are there hidden “to be solved later” gaps in core behavior?\n112\t\n113\t## Preferred review output structure\n114\t\n115\tUse:\n116\t\n117\t1. `Findings`\n118\t2. `Improvements I would consider`\n119\t3. `Assessment`\n120\t\n121\tUnder `Findings`:\n122\t\n123\t- order by severity\n124\t- use precise statements\n125\t- include file references\n126\t- say exactly what is wrong and what must change\n127\t\n128\tUnder `Improvements I would consider`:\n129\t\n130\t- only include real alternatives that could better improve proof time\n131\t- distinguish these from blockers\n132\t\n133\tUnder `Assessment`:\n134\t\n135\t- state whether there is a remaining design-level blocker\n136\t- state whether the plan is prototype-ready\n137\t- say what the strongest part of the plan is\n138\t\n139\t## Writing rules\n140\t\n141\t- Be concrete, not rhetorical.\n142\t- Prefer “As written, X is wrong because Y in current code does Z.”\n143\t- Do not say “looks good overall” before findings.\n144\t- Do not invent performance numbers.\n145\t- Do not call something “overengineering” unless you explain what simpler path exists.\n146\t- Do not let “future work” hide a present correctness or implementation gap.\n147\t- If the proposal is good, say so plainly after checking it.\n148\t\n149\t## Useful review heuristics\n150\t\n151\t- Cached data must include every static input needed to rebuild current behavior.\n152\t- Reuse scoped inside a function call does not help if the costly repetition happens across calls.\n153\t- Exact-size host copies matter when buffer APIs do not track logical length separately.\n154\t- A plan that ignores fallback paths is usually incomplete.\n155\t- A measured `~100us` span is not a serious optimization target inside a `~10ms+` phase.\n156\t- The cleanest prototype path is usually the one that matches an existing pattern already used elsewhere in the codebase.\n157\t\n158\t## Minimum bar before approving a prototype plan\n159\t\n160\tApprove only if all of these are true:\n161\t\n162\t- no remaining design-level correctness blocker\n163\t- no missing data needed to reproduce current behavior\n164\t- no hidden interface gap between Rust and CUDA\n165\t- the intended win targets a measured bottleneck\n166\t- the checklist is sufficient for someone else to implement the prototype without guessing\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BREvi9ZdCNL121MQfRpHhk","session_id":"f75a9bb0-08dc-4cc2-8653-93cb45f6f821","uuid":"054dc6c5-3844-410a-95bf-06858417f34e","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:20:46.028Z"}
